Static Casting and Dynamic Casting

1) Static Casting 
-> Static cast is an explicit, done at compile time conversion.
- Basic datatype conversion
syntax:
static_cast<new_type>(expression)
e.g. 
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int main()
{
    double x = 10.75;

    int y = static_cast<int>(x);

    cout << y;

    return 0;
}
// if i do int y = (int)x; // also works but
C-style casting works, but static_cast is preferred in modern C++ because it is type-safe, readable, compile-time checked, and avoids accidental unsafe conversions
safe for upcasting.     

conclude: 
static_cast is used for compile-time type conversion in C++. It is commonly used for datatype conversion and inheritance-based casting. It is faster but does not perform runtime checking, so downcasting using static_cast can be unsafe.

2) dynamic_cast
it is use for safe untime conversion.
mainly use in runtime polymorphism.

Base class must contain at least one virtual function.
syntax: dynamic_cast<new_type>(expression)

safe downcasting:
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Base
{
public:
    virtual void show()
    {
        cout << "Base class";
    }
};

class Derived : public Base
{
public:
    void display()
    {
        cout << "\nDerived display";
    }
};

int main()
{
    Base *b = new Derived();

    Derived *d = dynamic_cast<Derived*>(b);

    if (d != NULL)
    {
        d->display();
    }
    else
    {
        cout << "Casting Failed";
    }

    return 0;
}

Failed casting e.g.
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

class Base
{
public:
    virtual void show() {}
};

class Derived : public Base
{
};

class Test : public Base
{
};

int main()
{
    Base *b = new Test();

    Derived *d = dynamic_cast<Derived*>(b);

    if (d == NULL)
    {
        cout << "Casting Failed";
    }

    return 0;
}


Easy Interview Answer
static_cast

static_cast is used for compile-time type conversion. It is fast but does not perform runtime checking.

dynamic_cast

dynamic_cast is used for safe runtime type conversion in inheritance. It checks object type during runtime and works only with polymorphic classes.


