c) weak_ptr
Non-owning smart pointer.
Works with:
shared_ptr
Does NOT increase reference count.
e.g. 
#include <memory>
shared_ptr<int> sp(new int(50));
weak_ptr<int> wp = sp;

To solve, Circular dependency problem
means when, If both use shared_ptr, Reference count never becomes zero
Memory leak occurs
so use weak_ptr on one side.
e.g.
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>

using namespace std;

class B;

class A
{
public:
    shared_ptr<B> ptrB;

    ~A()
    {
        cout << "A Destructor Called" << endl;
    }
};

class B
{
public:
    weak_ptr<A> ptrA; // before here shared_ptr use so, boths ref. count increse never become zero cause memory leaked happen, object never destroyed. But use weak_ptr does NOT increase reference count. 
    //So memory releases properly.
    /* hence we say weak_ptr Does Not Own Memory It only:Observes object */

    ~B()
    {
        cout << "B Destructor Called" << endl;
    }
};

int main()
{
    shared_ptr<A> a(new A());
    shared_ptr<B> b(new B());

    a->ptrB = b;
    b->ptrA = a;
    return 0;
}
/* weak_ptr is used with shared_ptr to avoid circular references. It does not increase the reference count of the object. When two objects reference each other using only shared_ptr, memory leaks can occur because the reference count never becomes zero. weak_ptr solves this problem by keeping a non-owning reference to the object. */

Q14) What is RAII?
-> Resource Acquisition Is Initialization
RAII stands for Resource Acquisition Is Initialization. It is a C++ design principle where resources are acquired in constructors and released automatically in destructors. This ensures proper cleanup and exception safety. RAII is widely used in modern C++ through smart pointers, STL containers, file streams, and lock management to prevent memory leaks and improve resource handling.

Without RAII:
-Manual cleanup required
-Memory leaks possible
-Exception handling difficult

RAII provides:
-Automatic cleanup
-Safer code
-Better resource management


RAII principle-
Constructor: Allocated memory
Destructor: Automatically released memory

what is Rule of 3/5
-> Rule of 3 states that if a class manages dynamic resources and defines any one of destructor, copy constructor, or copy assignment operator, then it should probably define all three.
Rule of 5 extends this concept in C++11 by additionally including move constructor and move assignment operator for efficient resource transfer and performance optimization.

